     before acting on any DONE.
  3. [1mTask review[0m — a reviewer subagent gets the brief, the report, a generated diff package, and the plan's global
     constraints. It returns two verdicts: spec compliance and code quality. Both are required.
  4. [1mFix loop if needed[0m — up to five rounds per task. Rounds 1–3 resume the original implementer with the findings
     verbatim; rounds 4–5 dispatch a fresh implementer on a more capable model. Each round ends in a [3mscoped[0m re-review of
     just the fix diff. Minor findings get deferred to the ledger rather than looping. Findings that conflict with the
     plan's own text come to you to adjudicate — I don't dismiss them and I don't override the plan unilaterally.
  5. [1mCodex gate[0m — under Claude Code, an optional second-opinion code review per task (skipped for tasks the plan
     declares low-risk, if the plan itself was gate-reviewed). Its findings share the same five-round budget. Degrades
     cleanly if Codex isn't installed.
  6. [1mComplete[0m — append a completion line to the ledger with the commit range.

  If round five still leaves blocking findings, the task is surfaced to you as BLOCKED rather than continuing.

  [1mFinal[0m

  One whole-branch review on the most capable model, pointed at the deferred-minor list. If it finds things: exactly [1mone
[0m  fix wave (one subagent with the complete findings list, not one per finding), one scoped re-review, then anything
  still open comes to you. Final Codex gate, delete the scratch workspace, hand off to [38;5;153mfinishing-a-development-branch[39m.

  [1mTradeoffs worth knowing before you commit[0m

  - [1mIt runs continuously.[0m Once started, I don't check in between tasks — only for BLOCKED status, plan conflicts, or
    completion. That's deliberate, but it means you see the work in fewer, larger chunks.
  - [1mIt costs more tokens than inline execution.[0m Every task pays for an implementer plus a reviewer, and fix rounds add
    more. The payoff is that no task inherits another task's context pollution.
  - [1mIt fits mostly-independent tasks.[0m Tightly coupled tasks where each needs the previous one's full reasoning are a
    poor fit — for those, inline execution ([38;5;153mhyperpowers:executing-plans[39m) is better.
  - [1mSmall same-shape tasks get batched[0m into one dispatch rather than one agent each.

  Your global CLAUDE.md sets SDD as the default for executing a written plan, so if you want the [38;5;153mauth-system[39m plan in
  this repo executed, I'd go straight to it. Say the word and I'll start; or if you'd prefer inline execution for this
  one, just ask for that instead.

